Yes—if forgetting is continuous, then remembering stops being a one-time retrieval and becomes a continuous act of reconstruction.

You could frame it as:

\text{forgetting} \rightarrow \text{gap} \rightarrow \text{reconstruction} \rightarrow \text{remembering}

So in a constant state of forgetting, you are repeatedly rebuilding continuity from traces: context, habits, symbols, other people, environment, notes, bodily state, pattern recognition.

That makes memory less like a vault and more like a process that keeps re-instantiating the self.

And there’s a neat inversion in it: perfect retention might require very little remembering, because nothing ever has to be recovered. Forgetting is what creates the need for remembrance.

So perhaps:

To forget is to create the possibility of remembering.
To keep forgetting is to keep remembering.

Not necessarily accurately, though—that’s the important caveat. Reconstruction can preserve the underlying pattern while altering details. Which is why provenance, external anchors, and comparison across reconstructions matter when truth matters.